-- Focused search-quality improvements for five priority calculator pages.
-- Each page keeps its existing route and renderer while receiving distinct,
-- method-aligned metadata and editorial content.

UPDATE calculators
SET description = 'Measure car, bike, CNG or EV efficiency from trip distance or odometer readings, then calculate energy cost per kilometre and per 100 kilometres.',
    seo_title = 'Mileage Calculator – km/L, Fuel Cost & Odometer',
    seo_description = 'Calculate car or bike mileage from distance or odometer readings. See km/L, km/kg or km/kWh, fuel cost per kilometre and trip expense.',
    editorial_intro = 'Measure efficiency from a known distance and the fuel or electricity used over that same distance. For petrol and diesel, a repeatable tank-to-tank test is more useful than one short trip: refill to the same automatic cut-off, record the distance, and compare several fills under similar conditions.',
    benchmark_context_json = json('{"title":"Tank-to-tank measurement checklist","badge":"Repeatable vehicle measurement","stat":"Distance divided by fuel used","description":"Use the same unit and the same fill method at both ends of the test. Compare several complete refills rather than treating one short journey as the vehicle average.","source":"DevCalc calculation methodology","lastUpdated":"October 2026"}'),
    formula_json = json('{"title":"Mileage and running-cost formulas","formula":"Distance = Current odometer − Previous odometer; Efficiency = Distance ÷ Fuel or energy used; Cost per km = Unit price ÷ Efficiency","explanation":"Petrol and diesel results are shown in km/L, CNG in km/kg, and electric-vehicle efficiency in km/kWh. The price field is optional. When it is supplied, total cost equals units consumed multiplied by the unit price, and cost per kilometre equals total cost divided by distance.","example":{"input":"Current odometer 12,920 km; previous odometer 12,500 km; fuel added 28 L; fuel price ₹100/L","output":"Distance 420 km; mileage 15 km/L; fuel cost ₹2,800; fuel-only cost ₹6.67/km"},"useCases":["Tank-to-tank mileage checks","Bike and car efficiency tracking","CNG efficiency in km/kg","EV efficiency in km/kWh","Trip fuel or electricity budgeting"]}'),
    steps_json = json('[{"step":1,"title":"Choose the matching method","description":"Use trip distance when it is already known, or enter previous and current odometer readings to calculate the distance.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":2,"title":"Enter consumption for the same trip","description":"Add litres, kilograms of CNG, or kWh used over exactly the distance entered above.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":3,"title":"Add the current unit price if needed","description":"Enter the price per litre, kilogram, or kWh only when you want fuel-only running cost and trip expense.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":4,"title":"Compare like-for-like readings","description":"Review efficiency and cost, then repeat the measurement across similar routes or full tanks before judging a trend.","icon":"result"}]'),
    faqs_json = json('[{"question":"How do I calculate mileage from two odometer readings?","answer":"Subtract the previous odometer reading from the current reading to get distance, then divide that distance by the fuel added for the same interval. For example, 12,920 minus 12,500 is 420 km; 420 divided by 28 litres is 15 km/L."},{"question":"What is the most repeatable tank-to-tank method?","answer":"Fill to the first automatic nozzle cut-off, record the odometer, drive normally, and refill to the same cut-off. Divide the distance by the fuel added at the second fill. Combining several full-tank intervals reduces the effect of pump and fill variation."},{"question":"Does the calculator support CNG and electric vehicles?","answer":"Yes. Select CNG for a km/kg result or EV for km/kWh. The arithmetic is still distance divided by units consumed, but the units are different and should not be compared as though litres, kilograms, and kWh were interchangeable."},{"question":"How is fuel cost per kilometre calculated?","answer":"When a unit price is entered, the calculator multiplies consumption by price for total trip cost and divides that cost by distance. At ₹100 per litre and 15 km/L, the fuel-only running cost is about ₹6.67 per kilometre."},{"question":"Why can one mileage reading be unusually high or low?","answer":"Short fills, different nozzle cut-offs, idling, traffic, route, speed, load, tyre pressure, weather and air-conditioning can change a single result. Compare several readings collected with the same method before treating a difference as a trend."},{"question":"Does cost per kilometre include every ownership expense?","answer":"No. It includes only the fuel, CNG, or electricity represented by the entered unit price. Servicing, tyres, insurance, tolls, parking, finance costs and depreciation must be added separately for a total ownership-cost estimate."}]'),
    seo_content = '<h2>Calculate mileage from a trip or odometer readings</h2><p>The calculator supports two evidence-based inputs. If the trip distance is known, divide it by the fuel or energy consumed. If you recorded two odometer values, first subtract the earlier reading from the later reading. Both methods produce the same efficiency calculation when the distance and consumption cover the same journey.</p><h2>Worked tank-to-tank example</h2><p>A previous odometer reading of 12,500 km and a current reading of 12,920 km give a distance of 420 km. If the second refill adds 28 litres, mileage is 420 ÷ 28 = 15 km/L. At ₹100 per litre, the refill costs ₹2,800 and the fuel-only running cost is ₹2,800 ÷ 420 = ₹6.67 per km.</p><h2>Use the correct unit</h2><p>Petrol and diesel efficiency is normally expressed as km/L, CNG as km/kg, and electric efficiency as km/kWh. Each result answers how far the vehicle travelled per unit consumed. Do not compare the numeric values directly without also considering the price and energy represented by each unit.</p><h2>Why repeated readings are better</h2><p>A single short trip can be distorted by idling, traffic, refill variation, route and weather. For a tank-to-tank check, fill to the first automatic cut-off, record the odometer, drive normally, and refill using the same method. Combine distance and fuel across multiple fills for a more stable average.</p><h2>What the cost result includes</h2><p>The cost fields cover only the entered fuel or electricity price. They do not include maintenance, tyres, insurance, tolls, parking, financing or depreciation. Keep those costs separate when comparing total vehicle ownership.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'mileage-calculator';

UPDATE calculators
SET description = 'Enter two names to generate a repeatable love percentage from the exact spelling entered. The result is a private entertainment game, not relationship advice.',
    seo_title = 'Love Calculator by Name – Repeatable Percentage',
    seo_description = 'Enter two names for a repeatable love percentage based on letter values and a stable name hash. See the method, score band and entertainment-only limits.',
    editorial_intro = 'This name game converts the exact letters entered into a repeatable score. It does not evaluate attraction, consent, communication, values or relationship health, so a high or low result should be treated only as a playful conversation starter.',
    benchmark_context_json = json('{"title":"A repeatable name game, not a relationship test","badge":"Entertainment-only method","stat":"Same spellings, same score","description":"Names are normalized, converted to letter values and combined in a stable hash. Reversing the two names does not change the result.","source":"DevCalc Love Calculator implementation","lastUpdated":"October 2026"}'),
    formula_json = json('{"title":"How the love percentage is produced","formula":"Score = round((Numerology score + Stable name-hash score) ÷ 2), limited to 35–98","explanation":"Each normalized name receives a reduced A–Z letter-value total. The difference between those totals creates one component. The alphabetically sorted name pair also produces a stable hash component. Averaging them makes the same two spellings repeatable and order-independent; it does not measure real compatibility.","example":{"input":"Enter Rahul and Priya using the same spelling each time","output":"The calculator returns the same playful percentage even when the name order is reversed"},"useCases":["A two-name party game","Comparing nickname spellings","Sharing a light-hearted result","Checking that a result is repeatable"]}'),
    steps_json = json('[{"step":1,"title":"Enter the first name","description":"Use the first name, nickname, or spelling you want the game to process.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":2,"title":"Enter the second name","description":"Add the other name. Spaces, numbers and punctuation are ignored by the calculation.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":3,"title":"Generate the name score","description":"The calculator combines reduced letter values with a stable hash of the sorted name pair.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":4,"title":"Read it as entertainment","description":"Share or compare the repeatable result, but do not use it to judge a real relationship or another person.","icon":"result"}]'),
    faqs_json = json('[{"question":"Is the love percentage random?","answer":"No. The calculation is deterministic: the same two normalized spellings return the same score. Changing a spelling or nickname changes the text input and can therefore change the entertainment result."},{"question":"Does reversing the two names change the result?","answer":"No. The implementation sorts the normalized names before creating its stable hash, so entering A with B produces the same score as entering B with A."},{"question":"What information does the calculator actually use?","answer":"It uses only letters from the two names. It reduces A–Z letter values for one component and creates a stable hash from the sorted pair for another. It does not collect evidence about either person or the relationship."},{"question":"Can this score predict a successful relationship?","answer":"No. A name-based percentage cannot measure consent, trust, safety, communication, shared goals, attraction or future behaviour. Treat it as a game and base real decisions on the people and circumstances involved."},{"question":"Why does a nickname produce a different score?","answer":"A nickname changes the letters processed by both parts of the formula. Use the same spellings when you want a repeatable comparison; different spellings are intentionally different inputs."},{"question":"Are the entered names used for scientific matching?","answer":"No. The result is generated locally from text and is presented only as entertainment. It is not a psychological, astrological, medical or scientific compatibility assessment."}]'),
    seo_content = '<h2>A transparent two-name entertainment game</h2><p>The Love Calculator turns two spellings into a percentage that can be repeated. It first removes spaces, punctuation and non-letter characters. Each name receives a reduced A–Z letter-value total, while the sorted pair creates a second stable hash value. The two components are averaged and limited to the displayed score range.</p><h2>Why the same pair stays consistent</h2><p>The method contains no random draw. The exact normalized spellings create the same numeric inputs each time, and sorting makes the result independent of which person is entered first. A nickname can produce a different result because it contains different letters.</p><h2>What the percentage means</h2><p>The percentage is a label for a name game. It can be used for a party, group chat or playful comparison. It is not evidence of attraction or relationship quality, and a score band should not be interpreted as a prediction.</p><h2>What names cannot measure</h2><p>Real relationships depend on consent, respect, emotional safety, communication, shared expectations and behaviour over time. None of those facts is contained in a spelling. Do not use this result to pressure someone, settle a serious disagreement or make a relationship decision.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'love-calculator';

UPDATE calculators
SET description = 'Compare two names with a repeatable friendship score based on normalized character values. Use the result as a light-hearted BFF game, not a judgement of a friendship.',
    seo_title = 'Friendship Calculator by Name – BFF Score Test',
    seo_description = 'Enter two names for a repeatable friendship percentage and BFF score. See how the character-value method works and what the playful result cannot measure.',
    editorial_intro = 'This calculator sorts the letters in two normalized names, totals their character codes and maps the result into a score from 35 to 98. It is deliberately simple and repeatable; it cannot observe trust, loyalty, shared history or how friends treat one another.',
    benchmark_context_json = json('{"title":"Order-independent friendship name score","badge":"Transparent entertainment formula","stat":"Score = 35 + (character total mod 64)","description":"Only the normalized letters are used. Sorting the combined letters makes the output independent of name order.","source":"DevCalc Friendship Calculator implementation","lastUpdated":"October 2026"}'),
    formula_json = json('{"title":"Friendship score calculation","formula":"Friendship score = 35 + (sum of sorted lowercase character codes mod 64)","explanation":"The calculator removes non-letter characters, combines both names, sorts their letters, and adds the character-code value of every letter. The remainder after division by 64 is added to 35, producing a repeatable score from 35 to 98. This is a text transformation for entertainment, not a social assessment.","example":{"input":"Enter the same two name spellings in either order","output":"Both orders return the same percentage because the combined letters are sorted before scoring"},"useCases":["A BFF name game","Friend-group comparisons","Testing full names versus nicknames","Sharing a repeatable novelty score"]}'),
    steps_json = json('[{"step":1,"title":"Enter your chosen spelling","description":"Type the first name, nickname, or initials you want included in the game.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":2,"title":"Add the second name","description":"Use a consistent spelling when comparing results with a friend or group.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":3,"title":"Calculate the repeatable score","description":"The tool normalizes and sorts the letters, totals their character values, and maps the total to 35–98.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":4,"title":"Keep the result in context","description":"Use the percentage as a playful prompt; judge a friendship through trust, respect, communication and shared experience.","icon":"result"}]'),
    faqs_json = json('[{"question":"How is the friendship percentage calculated?","answer":"The tool keeps A–Z letters, combines and sorts both names, adds each character-code value, and maps the remainder into a score from 35 to 98. The method is repeatable but is not a scientific compatibility model."},{"question":"Will the same two names always return the same score?","answer":"Yes, when the spellings are unchanged. There is no random value in the implementation, so the same normalized inputs produce the same entertainment score each time."},{"question":"Does the order of the two names matter?","answer":"No. The letters from both normalized names are sorted together before the total is calculated. Reversing the names therefore leaves the combined character set and final score unchanged."},{"question":"Can I use nicknames or initials?","answer":"Yes, but every spelling is a different input. A nickname, full name and initials can return different values, so use the same format when making a playful comparison."},{"question":"Does a low result mean the friendship is weak?","answer":"No. The calculator cannot know shared experiences, trust, communication, reliability, boundaries or support. A low or high score only describes the output of the name formula and should not be used to label a real friendship."},{"question":"What actually helps a friendship remain strong?","answer":"Friendships are supported by mutual respect, honest communication, dependable behaviour, listening and healthy boundaries. Those qualities require human context and cannot be inferred from names."}]'),
    seo_content = '<h2>How this BFF name score is calculated</h2><p>The Friendship Calculator uses a deliberately simple text formula. It removes non-letter characters, converts both names to lowercase, combines and sorts the letters, then adds their character-code values. The remainder after division by 64 is added to 35, so the displayed range is 35 to 98.</p><h2>Why the answer is repeatable</h2><p>No random number is used. The same spellings create the same sorted characters and total every time. Sorting also means the order of the two people does not affect the result.</p><h2>Full names, nicknames and initials</h2><p>Each spelling is a distinct input. Rahul and Rahu do not contain the same character set, so they may not receive the same score. Agree on a naming format before comparing several pairs in a group.</p><h2>What a friendship percentage cannot assess</h2><p>A name formula cannot see loyalty, respect, communication, shared experience or boundaries. The percentage is suitable for casual entertainment, not for ranking friends or deciding whether a friendship is healthy.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'friendship-calculator';

UPDATE calculators
SET description = 'Estimate raw gold value from an entered rate, net weight and purity. Convert grams, kilograms, tola or troy ounces and avoid applying a purity adjustment twice.',
    seo_title = 'Gold Price Calculator – Value by Purity and Weight',
    seo_description = 'Estimate raw gold value from rate, net weight and 24K, 22K, 18K or 14K purity. Convert common units and see what making charges, GST and stones exclude.',
    editorial_intro = 'This calculator values only the metal represented by the rate, net weight and selected purity. Enter a current comparable quote and identify whether that quote is already for the selected karat. Jewellery invoices can also include making charges, GST, stones, hallmarking or seller-specific additions that are outside this raw-metal estimate.',
    benchmark_context_json = json('{"title":"Avoid applying purity twice","badge":"Rate-input check","stat":"24K base rate or selected-karat rate","description":"When the entered quote is already a 22K, 18K or 14K rate, enable the selected-purity-rate option. Otherwise the calculator treats the quote as a 24K base and applies the purity factor.","source":"DevCalc Gold Price Calculator methodology","lastUpdated":"October 2026"}'),
    steps_json = json('[{"step":1,"title":"Enter a current comparable rate","description":"Use a per-gram rate and note whether it represents 24K gold or the selected purity.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":2,"title":"Enter net metal weight","description":"Choose grams, kilograms, tola or troy ounces and exclude stones when checking raw gold value.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":3,"title":"Choose purity and rate basis","description":"Select 24K, 22K, 18K or 14K and indicate if the entered quote already matches that purity.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":4,"title":"Separate metal value from the final bill","description":"Review the estimated raw value, then account separately for making charges, GST, stones and seller deductions.","icon":"result"}]'),
    faqs_json = json('[{"question":"What does the Gold Price Calculator estimate?","answer":"It estimates raw metal value from the entered rate, converted weight and selected purity basis. It does not automatically reproduce a jewellery purchase invoice or a resale offer."},{"question":"Should I enter a 24K rate or a 22K rate?","answer":"Either can be used if the rate-basis option is set correctly. Leave the option off when entering a 24K base rate so the selected purity factor is applied. Turn it on when the quote already represents the selected karat."},{"question":"Why must the rate basis be identified?","answer":"Applying a 22K purity factor to a rate that is already quoted for 22K would reduce the value twice. The checkbox prevents that double adjustment and makes the calculation explicit."},{"question":"Are making charges and GST included?","answer":"No. The result is a raw metal estimate. Making charges, GST, stones, hallmarking, wastage or value-addition lines, discounts and seller-specific fees must be checked separately on an itemised invoice."},{"question":"How are tola and troy ounces converted?","answer":"The selected unit is converted to grams before value is calculated. Confirm the unit used by the seller because a troy ounce is not the same as an ordinary avoirdupois ounce, and local tola conventions may be rounded."},{"question":"Can I use this result as a resale value?","answer":"Not by itself. A buyer may apply purity testing, melting or handling deductions, exclude stones and use a different buyback rate. Ask for the weight, purity, rate and deductions in writing before comparing offers."},{"question":"Does DevCalc supply today’s gold rate?","answer":"No. Enter a current rate from the market or seller you are comparing. Gold rates vary by time, location, purity, transaction type and dealer, so an undated example should not be treated as a live quote."}]'),
    seo_content = '<h2>Estimate raw gold value, not a complete jewellery bill</h2><p>The calculation starts with an entered per-gram rate, converts the chosen weight unit to grams, and applies the selected purity only when the rate is a 24K base. The output is an estimate of metal value. It excludes making charges, GST, stones, hallmarking, wastage or value-addition lines, discounts and resale deductions.</p><h2>Choose the correct rate basis</h2><p>A seller may quote a 24K reference rate or a rate already adjusted for 22K, 18K or 14K. If the entered rate already matches the chosen purity, enable that option so the calculator does not apply purity twice. When using a 24K base rate, the tool applies its stored karat factor.</p><h2>Worked example with an illustrative rate</h2><p>For 10 g of 22K gold and an illustrative 24K base rate of ₹10,000 per gram, the stored factor is 0.916. Raw value is ₹10,000 × 10 × 0.916 = ₹91,600. Replace the example rate with a current comparable quote; it is not a live market price.</p><h2>Net weight and unit conversion</h2><p>Use net gold weight when jewellery contains stones or non-gold parts. The calculator converts kilograms, tola and troy ounces to grams before multiplication. Confirm the seller’s unit convention and written weight because rounding can change the estimate.</p><h2>Buying and resale require different checks</h2><p>A retail invoice can add charges and tax, while a resale buyer may use a lower buyback rate or deductions after purity testing. Compare itemised figures rather than treating raw metal value as the final amount payable or receivable.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'gold-price-calculator';

UPDATE calculators
SET description = 'Estimate body fat percentage with the U.S. Navy circumference method using height, neck, waist and, for women, hip measurements. Track trends consistently.',
    seo_title = 'Body Fat Calculator – U.S. Navy Tape Method',
    seo_description = 'Estimate body fat percentage with the U.S. Navy tape method for men and women. See the exact formula, measurement steps, category and important limits.',
    editorial_intro = 'The U.S. Navy circumference method estimates body fat from tape measurements rather than measuring fat directly. Use consistent landmarks and conditions when tracking a trend, and treat the result as a screening estimate rather than a diagnosis or exact laboratory value.',
    benchmark_context_json = json('{"title":"A tape-based estimate with measurement limits","badge":"Body-composition estimate","stat":"Repeat the same landmarks","description":"Small changes in tape placement, posture and breathing can change the result. Compare measurements taken with the same technique rather than focusing on one decimal place.","source":"U.S. Navy circumference-method equations","lastUpdated":"October 2026"}'),
    formula_json = json('{"title":"U.S. Navy circumference formulas used","formula":"Men: 86.010 × log10(waist in − neck in) − 70.041 × log10(height in) + 36.76; Women: 163.205 × log10(waist in + hip in − neck in) − 97.684 × log10(height in) − 78.387","explanation":"The interface accepts centimetres and converts them to inches before applying these inch-based constants. Men enter height, neck and waist. Women also enter hip circumference. The formula estimates body fat from population-derived relationships and does not directly scan body tissue.","example":{"input":"Male: height 175 cm, neck 40 cm, waist 85 cm","output":"The values are converted to inches before the Navy equation is applied; the result is an estimate and can vary with tape placement"},"useCases":["Consistent at-home trend tracking","Comparing repeated circumference measurements","Understanding body composition beyond scale weight","Preparing questions for a qualified health professional"]}'),
    steps_json = json('[{"step":1,"title":"Select the applicable formula","description":"Choose male or female because the circumference equations use different measurement combinations.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":2,"title":"Measure at consistent landmarks","description":"Enter height, neck and waist in centimetres; women also enter hip circumference. Keep the tape level and snug without compressing skin.","icon":"height"},{"step":3,"title":"Repeat before entering","description":"Take each circumference more than once under similar posture and breathing conditions, then resolve noticeably different readings.","icon":"calculator"},{"step":4,"title":"Interpret an estimate, not a diagnosis","description":"Use the value mainly for trends with the same method. Discuss unexpected or health-critical results with a qualified professional.","icon":"result"}]'),
    faqs_json = json('[{"question":"Which body-fat method does this calculator use?","answer":"It uses the U.S. Navy circumference equations. The interface accepts centimetres, converts them to inches, and applies sex-specific logarithmic formulas based on height and tape measurements."},{"question":"Where should I take the tape measurements?","answer":"Use a flexible non-stretch tape, keep it level and snug without compressing skin, and follow the calculator’s sex-specific landmarks. Measure in a relaxed posture after a normal exhale and repeat each site for consistency."},{"question":"Why can two measurements give different results?","answer":"Tape position, posture, breathing, time of day and rounding can shift circumference values. Repeat the same landmarks under similar conditions and focus on a trend rather than one decimal place."},{"question":"Is the Navy method as accurate as a clinical scan?","answer":"No. It is a practical circumference estimate and can differ from laboratory or imaging methods for an individual. Body shape and measurement technique affect the equation, so it should not be treated as a direct tissue measurement."},{"question":"How is body fat percentage different from BMI?","answer":"BMI uses only weight relative to height, while the Navy method uses circumference relationships to estimate fat percentage. Neither method directly diagnoses health, and each can misrepresent some body types."},{"question":"Can I use the category as a medical diagnosis or target?","answer":"No. Category bands are broad reference labels and vary by source, sex, age and population. Do not use one estimate to diagnose a condition or choose a restrictive diet; seek qualified advice for health decisions."},{"question":"What is the best way to track change over time?","answer":"Use the same tape, landmarks, posture and time-of-day conditions, and compare a series of readings rather than daily fluctuations. Keep method changes separate because different tools are not directly interchangeable."}]'),
    seo_content = '<h2>What this body-fat estimate measures</h2><p>The U.S. Navy circumference method uses relationships between height and body circumferences to estimate body fat percentage. It does not scan fat tissue and cannot distinguish visceral from subcutaneous fat. The result is most useful as a repeatable estimate when the same measurement technique is used over time.</p><h2>Inputs for men and women</h2><p>Men enter height, neck and waist. Women enter height, neck, waist and hip. The interface accepts centimetres and converts them to inches before applying the stored inch-based constants, so values should not be manually converted before entry.</p><h2>Measurement technique matters</h2><p>Use a flexible non-stretch tape held level and snug without pressing into the skin. Stand relaxed, measure after a normal exhale, and repeat each site. A small change in tape position can move the final estimate.</p><h2>Worked interpretation</h2><p>For a man entering 175 cm height, 40 cm neck and 85 cm waist, the tool converts each value to inches and applies the male logarithmic equation. The displayed percentage and category are estimates. Repeating the measurements consistently is more informative than treating one decimal place as exact.</p><h2>Body fat percentage and BMI answer different questions</h2><p>BMI compares weight with height and does not distinguish fat from muscle. A circumference method uses body-shape measurements but still relies on a population equation. Neither number alone diagnoses health or replaces an individual clinical assessment.</p><h2>How to use the result responsibly</h2><p>Track a series collected with the same method and conditions. If a result is unexpected, recheck the tape landmarks before drawing conclusions. Consult a qualified healthcare professional when the number will influence treatment, aggressive dieting or another health decision.</p>'
WHERE slug = 'body-fat-calculator';
